Fillets: a ball that stays put between two tubes of its radius is walked
with its section in the guide's normal plane (mitcad#1412).

Where both supports of a constant-radius blend are tubes of the ball's
radius whose axes run through its centre (two earlier roundings of that
radius meeting in a mitre), the centre seen from either contact does not
depend on the contact's place round its tube's section: BlendFunc_ConstRad's
conditions (the centres seen from both contacts one, the mid-point of the
contacts in the guide's normal plane) leave the section one freedom, their
Jacobian singular. The solvers' roots slide along it with their starts: the
inverse function took a walk's start past the guide's end with a contact
out of its face, the walk's contacts ran out of their faces or did not move
(Blend_SamePoints), and the approximation's solver did not converge between
the walk's points.

Every section of such a ball is an arc of its sphere. The section whose
contacts both lie in the guide's normal plane keeps the mid-point's
condition, is the rounding's own section where the walk arrives at the ball
along a rim, and vanishes where the mitre ends.

- BlendFunc_ConstRad::StillSection (new) gives it, each contact moved round
  its tube's section into the plane, keeping its parameter along the tube
  (that of another section of the ball where one is given). IsSolution and
  both Section overloads with derivatives take that section's derivatives
  (none where it starts at rest, the mitre leaving the edge whose rounding
  ends at the ball).
- BRepBlend_Walking takes that section for each step's point (the solver
  from it slid along the freedom, and along the tubes by its tolerance) and
  starts a walk at it, its contacts on the arcs where they lie on them,
  instead of a start the inverse function moved behind it. A contact that
  stays where it was on its face's boundary (the ball touching a face at
  one point at its edge with the next, while the guide runs round the
  other contact's tube) has not left the face, and the step is judged by
  the other contact: the points are not taken for confused.
- BRepBlend_AppFuncRoot takes that section at every parameter, the walk's
  own points too, with the parameters along the tubes of the line's middle
  point: solved again from the walk's points the sections slid, and taken
  from the line's ends (found otherwise, a little apart along the tubes)
  they did not join smoothly, and the approximation cut them up to its
  limit (1411 poles, the end 0.0146 off its contact).

The case: a lid whose rims are rounded with 1 through the mitres of
earlier roundings of 1 (a reported design's item; a sphere of 1 at each
corner in the stored body). With 1521, both rims' chains build, valid, the
faces within 2.5e-4 mm of the stored body's.
